you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  tributes from environmental groups and  conservationists around the world  for prince philip who's died at the age  of 99.  the very real impact of climate change  on people's lives we report from  guatemala where  food insecurity and extreme weather are  forcing thousands from their homes  and more calls for the world's biggest  climate summit to be delayed  again because of the kovit 19 pandemic  greta thundberg says cop 26  shouldn't go ahead unless vaccination  programs mean all countries can attend  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and today we start with the  news that the duke of edinburgh has  passed away at the age of 99.  throughout his life he was committed to  the conservation of nature  and the natural environment in 1961  prince philip co-founded the world  wildlife fund  now the world's largest conservation  organization  he was an early advocate for the  environment a cause he did much to  further  during his lifetime well our royal  correspondent rihanna mills joins me now  so prince philip rhiannon said to be  ahead of his time when it came to the  environment  that's right and i think with his death  of course we are remembering the  hundreds of different patronages and the  charities that he supported  and a large amount of that work  certainly a couple of decades ago was  around the environment as you said he  had those links with the world wildlife  fund  and he was dedicated to those issues of  conservation  and the environment of course in more  recent years we have  become so used to prince charles and  also more recently prince william  talking about those important  environmental issues and specifically  the damage that climate change is doing  to the planet  but i think for the younger royals  certainly that  first few steps that prince philip took  a few decades ago  was really an inspiration to them and i  spoke to prince william  last year when he launched his own earth  shot prize and he talked very movingly  about how prince philip was very much  ahead of his time how he would talk  about issues around  population growth and the damage that  that was doing and  the impact that that was having on the  planet prince philip also had these  ideas about the links between nature  and religion and was very keen to gather  religious groups  with conservationists to talk about  those issues and so as we reflect on the  impact that he's had  i think his environmental legacy will be  hugely important  as we look ahead over the next few days  and remember that remarkable legacy  rhiannon thank you and there's  continuing coverage of this story on our  website  app and social media channels  let's take a look now at the day's other  climate news and we're focusing on  central america where the effects of  climate change are forcing many to flee  north to the united states more than 200  000 guatemalan migrants were apprehended  by u.s border patrol  in 2019. people coming to the u.s  from the northern triangle that's  guatemala honduras and el salvador  now outnumber those from mexico in part  that's because of  this a region known as the dry corridor  where extreme droughts mean that one in  six  people experience food insecurity  skies chief correspondent stuart ramsey  reports from guatemala  he's tiny and he's starving but his  mother's milk simply doesn't have the  nutrition needed to feed him properly  jason gossel is two months old and  weighs just  five and a half pounds  [Music]  he should be double the size at least  next to him his two and a half year old  brother sleeps he's tiny as well  in this part of guatemala everyone is  hungry  just like the brothers there's a food  crisis here  that has gone on for years and it's  getting worse  volunteer aid workers a handful of them  like sofia latona  are all that stopping families like  jason's from starving to death  the reality is that his body is so frail  because of malnutrition  and everything that happens to him will  be a consequence of him being too weak  to fight back  the tell-tale signs of malnutrition are  [Music]  everywhere  with bloated stomachs get almost no help  there's very little their families can  do for them  little food and parasite infested water  is a deadly mix guatemala  now has one of the worst malnutrition  rates in the world  this quiet crisis is being caused by  climate change  the weather is now so variable growing  enough food  has become all but impossible  this is called the dry corridor it  stretches through the central american  countries of el salvador  honduras nicaragua and here in eastern  guatemala  the mountains and the valleys of the dry  corridor appear magnificent and green  but there's little in the way of crops  growing here  landslips caused by flooding are common  recent hurricanes have made it worse  many farmers have simply given up and  left  many joining so-called caravans heading  to the united states  environmental migrants not criminals  leaving homelands  that simply can't support them  well they have pretty meager crop yields  here as a rule  even in the best of times but now a  combination of  no rain intense heat  and a hurricane means that 100  of the local crop has been destroyed  they will get nothing this year  what they're saying is that the weather  is changing all the time  there's absolutely no guarantee when the  rains will come when the right sun will  be there  and then of course they're getting these  wild weather in the shape of a hurricane  and that is making life here really  really difficult  farmers like eduardo rocket try to hold  on  but he showed me his failing crop he's  fighting a losing battle  against the elements  these mountain communities are all  desperately poor all lacking in proper  food and water  but they share another trait there are  virtually no men  they've left to find work to feed their  families  and many never return  many of these children are likely to  find themselves environmental migrants  as well  born into an environment that can't  sustain them  mr ramsey sky news guatemala  in other climate news today the road to  cop is looking more uncertain after  greta tunberg said she won't be going to  glasgow for the summit in november  the environmental campaigner tweeted to  say the climate conference should be  postponed until  all countries can attend on the same  terms she believes any restrictions on  non-vaccinated people traveling  is undemocratic snow leopards lemurs and  peruvian pelicans could face extinction  if global warming trends continue  according to a new study published in  biological conservation  animals from some of the world's most  bio-diverse regions will  disappear if the planet heats up by more  than three degrees  but scientists say it's not too late to  turn it around  and children in england are being  encouraged to make their journeys more  eco-friendly the  government is investing 18 million  pounds on a cycling training scheme  to give young people the confidence to  ride a bike it'll focus on things like  how to tackle difficult junctions  and roundabouts so let's take you over  now to our data dashboard to take a look  at the real-time  power mix in great britain today that's  the figures up here and this shows how  much of our energy is coming from fossil  fuels  from renewables and from nuclear sources  and it shows just what a key role the  weather plays in influencing our  electricity generation  mix now yesterday from midday through to  the evening wind and solar output was  high and that  makes up the bulk of that renewables  figure generating around half of  britain's electricity  today though their output is lower  delivering about 25  of our electricity and that's due to  calmer and cloudier weather  and at the same time colder temperatures  around three degrees below seasonal  average  are driving higher demand for power  leading to a larger proportion of gas  to match supply with demand and you can  see our data dashboard at any time and  read more about what those numbers mean  on our website  that's at skynews.com climate  let's return to our main news now and  tributes have been paid to prince philip  who's died at the age of 99.  he was the first president of the  conservation society wwf uk  and they've tweeted to say we express  our condolences on the passing away  today of hrh  the duke of edinburgh president emeritus  of wwf  international prince philip's lifelong  passion for the environmental cause will  continue to guide us  as we work to protect and restore nature  for people  and the planet and alex sharma the  president of cop 26 summit tweeted  prince philip dedicated a lifetime to  selfless public service providing  wonderful support to her majesty the  queen and was a great champion  for the environment well joining me now  is martin palmer  former secretary general of arc an  environmental organization he founded  with prince philip  thanks very much indeed for talking to  us martin palmer can you describe to us  what prince philip's concerns were about  the environment and climate change  well i know his his concern about the  environment of course goes back a very  long way as you say he was there at the  foundation of wwf in 1961  and poured his energies his passions  into that  and it was out of that really that i  think his particular  approach towards climate change issues  emerged  his anxiety uh very much from the very  beginning when climate change was  beginning to dominate  uh the politics the economic economics  of the  of the environmental world was the  danger of losing sight  that climate change was not the only  issue  uh at one level his concern was that  climate change  i'm saying with biodiversity loss uh and  population issues which as you know was  a big push of his  were all symptoms of a deeper problem  which was that we were out of kilter  with the rest of nature and his  his anxiety about climate change was  that he feared and to some extent he was  right  that it would eclipse focus on oceans on  forests that it would eclipse  focus on the protection of particular  endangered species that it would  not give the same attention to the rest  of  nature as it would give to human needs  and that it will become a major  political  and economic um uh diversion  away from what he felt uh were the major  concerns which was that we needed to get  into a balanced relationship with nature  and so he was very concerned and  we had many many long conversations uh  about this  um he he was very anxious that it was  very anthropocentric  its focus was almost entirely what is  its impact going to be on human beings  um and so for example um when  last year last september september the  un held its first ever  uh summit on biodiversity he was  delighted because he felt  that along with the focus on climate  change and obviously the cop here  in in glasgow in november uh he felt  that  a balance was being restored he was very  concerned  that by focusing only and primarily only  on the impact on human beings  of climate change um we were in danger  of once again  assuming that the whole purpose of  nature was to support human life  and his view was no that is not the  whole purpose of nature  and we've seen of course prince charles  and prince william expressing their  concerns about the environment to what  extent do you think  he was an inspiration to his family on  the subject  i think a very great deal he was an  inspiration  um and i suppose to a certain extent  they also had to sort of find  uh particular niches for themselves  given how much he  was such a very major dominant figure  particularly in the 60s 70s  80s through to the 90s but there's no  question that the passion  particularly i think you see this in the  tusk trust  the their absolute passion to protect  the elephants to protect the most  endangered species  as well as to address other issues that  springs very much from him  martin palmer thank you pleasure  and that's it for today's show but you  can get your climate fix over the  weekend with our podcast  and on this week's episode find out why  dr freddie otto from the university of  oxford  thinks climate change won't be solved if  people feel guilty about flying  climate cast is available wherever you  normally get your podcast from  and of course you can watch our weekly  digital show with ashna harinag on sky  news social channels our app and our  website  thanks for watching and we'll see you  next week

